      "text": "From $x + 3y = 3$, we can express $x$ in terms of $y$ as $x = 3 - 3y.$ Then we plug $x$ into the absolute value equation and we need to split it up into three separate cases to solve:\n1. When $x + y \\geq 0$ and $x - y \\geq 0$, that gives us $|x| - |y| = (x + y) - (y - x) = 0$ and $y = 1$.\n2. When $x + y \\geq 0$ and $x - y \\leq 0$, that gives us $|x| - |y| = (x + y) - (-y + x) = 2y$ and $y = .\\frac{1}{3}$.\n3. When $x + y \\leq 0$ and $x - y \\leq 0$, that gives us $|x| - |y| = (y + x) - (y - x) = 0$ and $y = -1.$\nSince $x = 3 - 3y$ in all three cases, we plug them in: \n1. $x = 3 - 3*1 = 0$, giving us $(0, 1).$\n2. $x = 3 - 3*. \\frac{1}{3} = 2$, giving us $(2, .\\frac{1}{3}).$\n3. $x = 3 - 3*-1 = 6$, giving us $(6, -1).$\nThat means there are $\\boxed{3}$ solutions. The answer is 3",
